  "strongest": {
    "chapter": "draft/22-nine-numbered.md",
    "quote": "The nine is in the left margin. It is in the column with the other numbers, at the same distance from the left edge, the same height. It is not a bracket.",
    "argument": "Twenty-one chapters of preparation pay out here. The sentence lands as both personal devastation and a completed logical argument because Chapter 9 has already taught the reader exactly why a bracket and a numbered line are different orders of information. The bookkeeper's discipline is not stripped away at the emotional climax — it is what the emotional climax is made of. A competent-but-ordinary novel stages this as discovery; this one stages it as confirmation of an audit."
  },
  "weakest": {
    "chapter": "draft/16-two-branches.md",
    "argument": "Formally necessary but the fifth variation on a three-note sequence. The relay mechanism — trusted acquaintance reaches a node Ilinca cannot approach; response travels back through a child or a folded slip — has been established and executed twice before. The new content is added to an accounting structure the reader is already tracking without friction, so the chapter moves without tension. The prose is still good; the chapter is doing work the novel has already done."
  },
  "attention_flags": "Attention begins to drift around Chapter 13 (The Bloc Woman) and reaches its most laborious point in Chapter 16 (Two Branches), where the accumulation of indirect relay contacts runs on the same rails without adding new emotional or narrative material. The drag extends roughly through Chapter 17. This is a structural weight problem, not a prose quality problem.",
  "comprehension": "The subject — illegal abortion aftercare in communist Romania, and the tracing of a hand-copied document — becomes clear by Chapter 4. The transmission-node numbering system requires some tracking but never produces genuine confusion. The three-perspective structure is immediately distinguishable throughout.",
  "reading_experience": "Effortful but rewarding. Dense, precise, demanding of sustained attention. The sentences pay back the effort they cost. Not a slog at any point. Closer in texture to Sebald than to any thriller.",
  "would_feel_annoyed": false,
  "jacket_matters": "If the jacket promises historical literary fiction set in communist Romania, the book delivers that precisely and you finish satisfied. If it promises something closer to a thriller, the middle third would frustrate — this novel generates tension through precision and patience, not plot mechanics. A general reader expecting the former is well-served; one expecting the latter is usually won over by Chapter 5.",
  "full_review": "The airport test runs as follows: you pick it up because the cover implies something serious about women in communist Romania. You read the first page — a bookkeeper alone in an office in November, feeding a mechanical adding machine, waiting for a corridor to empty — and you note that the prose is unusually controlled. You keep reading because the sentences are good and because something is clearly being hidden with great care. By Chapter 4 (Munteanu visiting the Petrescu household and collecting evidence envelopes) you are fully committed, and from that point you do not look up. The middle section (roughly Chapters 13-17) is the exception: the relay structure Ilinca uses to trace transmission branches — bread-queue acquaintance reaches pharmacy contact, bakery delivery boy carries a folded note, child delivers a message on the way to school — is inventive and historically credible, but by Chapter 16 it has been executed three times and the reader understands the mechanism before Ilinca deploys it again. This is where I began to read slightly faster. I report this honestly as data.\n\nThe central formal conceit is brilliant and earned: Ilinca wrote the document in October 1983 with the ninth element set in a hand-drawn bracket outside the numbered list. When she copied it by hand in a coal cellar in bad light thirteen months later, she gave the bracket a number. It became line nine. A careful copyist two hands further down read nine numbered lines and put the ninth in sequence with the eighth, which mentioned 'morning,' and wrote: take her in in the morning. The original bracket had said: Do not wait for the morning. Ana Petrescu arrived at the hospital in the morning.\n\nThis is not a thriller. The novel does not conceal its mechanism — Chapter 9 states the argument plainly: 'The bracket is not a step in a sequence. It is a different order of information entirely.' What it does instead is build a 30-chapter structure in which the confirmation of this argument is devastating rather than merely conclusive. When Ilinca finally reads the copy she made in the coal cellar, standing in a stairwell on Strada Crișan, the moment has been prepared through 21 chapters of patient accounting. The nine she reads hits because of accumulated weight, not because of surprise.\n\nMunteanu is the thinnest of the three narrators. His procedural chapters are gripping — the close-reading of textual variants across different copies is genuinely tense — but he is less emotionally present on the page than either woman. He functions as the novel's mechanism of understanding rather than as a full person. This is a real weakness.\n\nBut the final third recovers everything lost in the middle. The convergence — Ilinca's decision, Munteanu working toward the same address, the December 5 deadline — is handled with the precision that characterizes the novel's best work. The ending, Doina on the Someș bank reciting the document to the fog and getting the bracket right — 'I had my coat. I had my buletin. I had the nine elements in my head, all nine, in order, the bracket included' — closes the formal argument and the emotional arc at the same moment. This is a book that a smart general reader will keep reading and finish glad they did."
}

